<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Himalayas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Himalaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Himalayas are the highest mountain range on Earth, stretching about 2,400 kilometres from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and Bhutan, between the Tibetan Plateau and the plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. They contain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-everest">Mount Everest</a> and many of the world's highest peaks. The range has shaped the climate, rivers and history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, while its passes carried trade, pilgrims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> between India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/himalayas.jpg" alt="Himalayas" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Landsat image · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himalayas_and_allied_ranges_NASA_Landsat_showing_the_eight_thousanders,_annotated_with_major_rivers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
